  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn area of high clouds will pass over the area this morning, through \nmidday or early afternoon. Some filtered sunshine will likely make \nit through the rather thin high clouds, with more sun expected by \nlate afternoon as the high clouds exit east. It will be warm, with \nhighs in the 60s for most locations. Remaining mild tonight as a \nsteady south wind develops. Temperatures only dip down into the \nupper 30s and 40s areawide, under mainly clear skies. \n\nSunny skies prevail on Thursday and southwest winds increase between \n7-15 mph. This combined with 850mb temperatures reaching +10C will \nallow for high temperatures in the upper 60s to mid-70s, which is \nabout 15 degrees above average for the end of October. \n\nA weak frontal boundary approaches Thursday evening into Thursday \nnight from the north. This will bring increasing clouds and a few \nshowers; mainly along and north of I-90. Rainfall amounts will be \nvery light. From the Twin Tiers south into NE PA no rain is \nexpected. After the dry cold front moves through there could be some \nlingering morning clouds over Central NY Friday morning. Otherwise, \nmore sunshine is expected along with breezy west-northwest winds, \nwith gusts up to 25 mph. It will be cooler, with highs in the mid-\n50s to low 60s, except still mid 60s for the Wyoming Valley...this \nis still slightly above average for this time of year. High pressure \nbuilds overhead Friday night, Saturday and right into Sunday. This \nwill set the stage for a seasonable, dry and exceptional fall \nweather weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWhile overall moisture and cloud cover may begin to increase on \nMonday, most of the deterministic model guidance is trending drier \ninitially. With that said, the ensemble guidance and WPC still \nbrings a lower chance for light rain showers Monday and Tuesday next \nweek, with increase chances by the middle of next week. Exact \nfrontal and shower timing remains to be seen, but eventually it \nappears our dry weather streak will come to an end sometime next \nweek. Temperatures look to remain seasonably mild next week, with \nhighs in the upper 50s to mid-60s and overnight lows generally in \nthe upper 30s to 40s for most locations.",
